course: dictionary
lesson: 3859
title: Foxgloves: beautiful but poisonous
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A foxglove is a tall plant with bell-shaped flowers, purple, pink or white, that hang down one side of the stem. You can find them in woods, hedges and gardens. Looking at the flowers is safe, but eating any part of the plant is not.
explain: «Foxgloves are poisonous, but the medicine digitalis made from them has helped heart patients since the doctor William Withering wrote about it in 1785.» Poisonous means it can harm you if you eat it. The word but warns you of a twist: the plant is harmful, yet people learned to use it carefully.
explain: «Do not put wild plants or flowers in your mouth; foxgloves, daffodils and buttercups are poisonous.» The clue is the word poisonous. Every part of a foxglove is poisonous, so the rule is simple: never eat it, and wash your hands after touching it.
explain: «Plants cannot walk to each other, so they hire couriers and pay them in food: a bumblebee visiting foxgloves drinks sweet nectar, and comes out with yellow pollen on its legs and furry body.» The bees are the couriers. They climb right inside the flowers to collect nectar, and carry pollen to the next flower.
fact: A foxglove is a tall garden and wild plant with bell-shaped flowers.
fact: «Poisonous» means it can make you ill or hurt you if you eat it.
fact: Every part of a foxglove is poisonous, so never eat it, and wash your hands after touching it.
fact: Foxgloves grow in woods, hedges and gardens in the UK, Ireland, New Zealand and parts of North America and Australia.
fact: A medicine for the heart, digitalis, was first made from foxgloves, and it shows that the dose matters.
exercise: What is a foxglove? | A kind of stone found by rivers | A small bird that sings in hedges | *A tall garden and wild plant with bell-shaped flowers hanging down one side of its stem | A type of mushroom that grows in woods
exercise: «Never eat a berry, seed or leaf unless a grown-up says it is safe.» Why? | Berries are always sweet and safe | *Plants like foxglove and yew are poisonous | Grown-ups want to keep all the plants for themselves | Leaves are full of vitamins only
exercise: What should you do after touching a foxglove? | Put your hands in your mouth | Rub your eyes | *Wash your hands | Eat a leaf to test it
test: «A bumblebee visiting foxgloves drinks sweet nectar.» What do the bees get from the foxglove flowers? | Poison | Feathers | Stones | *Sweet nectar
test: In «Foxgloves are poisonous, but the medicine ... has helped heart patients», what does the word but show? | Two plants that are the same | A new flower has appeared | *A twist: the plant is harmful, yet a medicine comes from it | A question someone is asking
exam: What does «poisonous» mean in «Foxgloves are poisonous»? | *Able to make you ill or hurt you if you eat it | Safe to eat with a grown-up's help | Pretty and bright in colour | Tall and thin
